Alright, I need to keep digging into the TUI rendering logic to make sure the streaming output isn’t corrupting the rest of the UI. So far I’ve tackled the visible‑prefix handling and added an overflow prop to the outer Box, which should stop the frame from spilling over when lines wrap. That was a good first pass, but I’m still seeing potential issues when the component updates its list of items.

Looking at the source, the component currently maintains a single `items` state array:

```tsx
const [items, setItems] = useState<Item[]>([]);
```

All UI elements—both the static intro text (the welcome banner, any pre‑loaded help, etc.) and the live streaming chunks that get appended as the model generates output—are pushed into this same array. When the stream grows, the component re‑renders, and because the height calculations are based on the total number of rows, wrapped lines from the live stream can push the static content off the visible region. Moreover, the logic that decides how many rows to keep (the budget loop I just added) walks the array backwards, counting rows for each line. If static and live items are intermingled, it’s hard to guarantee that the static intro stays intact while the live part scrolls.

A cleaner approach would be to separate the two concerns: keep a `staticItems` array for everything that should remain fixed (intro, help, any pre‑generated content) and a `liveItems` array for the incremental output that streams in during a generation. This way the rendering loop can treat them independently—e.g., always render `staticItems` first, then apply the budget‑capped logic only to `liveItems`. That should prevent the overflow bug from re‑introducing itself when the live stream pushes the UI past its height.

I need to modify the state declarations accordingly. The existing block is:

```tsx
  const [phase, setPhase] = useState<"intro" | "ready">(
    opts.intro && config.intro ? "intro" : "ready",
  );
  const [items, setItems] = useState<Item[]>([]);
```

I’ll replace it with three lines: keep `phase` as is, then introduce `staticItems` and `liveItems` with their own setters. I’ll preserve the original initialization (empty arrays) and keep the same variable names for later code to reference. The new block should look like:

```tsx
  const [phase, setPhase] = useState<"intro" | "ready">(
    opts.intro && config.intro ? "intro" : "ready",
  );
  const [staticItems, setStaticItems] = useState<Item[]>([]);
  const [liveItems, setLiveItems] = useState<Item[]>([]);
```

I’ll make sure to set `replace_all` to false so only this exact snippet gets swapped, avoiding unintended changes elsewhere. This edit aligns with the recent overflow fix and sets the stage for updating the rendering logic to handle the two item streams separately.

